  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 0,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In May of this year, Meta announced it \nwould be laying off around 8,000 employees,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3920,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or around 10% of its entire workforce. Just \n2 months before that, it laid off a further  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 8480,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "700 people, and 2 months before that, it \nwas another 1,500 people. Again, in 2025,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14480,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it laid off a further 4,000 people, including \na large chunk from its AI division. And this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 19520,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was all on top of the record layoffs from its \nyear of efficiency, which actually lasted 3  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 24080,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "years and cut an additional 26,000 jobs. Now, \nobviously, this hasn't been great for morale,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29920,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even after the Zuck mandated that people start \nhaving fun again, but if you do the math,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34480,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that's collectively around 40,000 people \nthat have been laid off over the last 4  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 38320,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and 1/2 years. All from a company that only had \naround 75,000 employees to begin with. For most  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 43680,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the other major tech companies, the numbers \naren't really much better. And in specific cases,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48400,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they are actually much worse. This also isn't \nincluding the people who have just, you know,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53440,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "made their you money and quit. Now, obviously, \nbig tech in particular has been changing rapidly.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 59120,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Companies are investing hundreds of billions of \ndollars into AI development, which means they  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 62960,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just have less free cash flow to pay their staff \nand a greater incentive to justify that very same  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 67920,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "investment by doing supposedly AI enabled layoffs. \nBut if this is really happening so broadly,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73920,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how many jobs do these companies really have left \na cut? Well, the good news or the inconvenient  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 79120,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "truth is uh basically just as many as they \nstarted with. Apart from the outliers like X,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85120,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "most of the major tech companies have just as many \npeople today as they did when layoffs became the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 89600,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hot new trend in the valley. The same is true \nfor more traditional industries as well that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 93840,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have quietly had to curb their layoff enthusiasm. \nAnd this does raise the obvious question of why  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 99440,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bother? These companies have effectively passed \nthe same group of technical talent around between  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 104000,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one another, killed the vibes that they so \nclearly covet, undermined the job security  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107840,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that made tech jobs so desirable in the first \nplace, all to generate headlines that didn't  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 111920,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "really look as good as they thought they would. \nSo, what was all of this for? This week alone,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 118400,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about a dozen major companies announced job \ncuts, including Amazon and Google's parent,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 123280,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Alphabet. Meta announced Thursday that it's laying \noff about 8,000 workers as it continues to ramp up  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 128560,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spending on artificial intelligence. In a dramatic \ntwist, Open AI Sam Alman has revealed that Meta is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 134560,
    "dDurationMs": 8400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dangling jaw-dropping $100 million signing bonuses \nto lure his top engineers. 76% of Nvidia employees  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142960,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are millionaires and 37% are worth over $20 \nmillion. How crazy is that? Ford has rehired  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150880,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "350 experienced engineers after admitting that \nartificial intelligence alone did not deliver  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 156080,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the quality it expected in vehicle development. \nOkay, so even if you set aside the very real human  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 163440,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cost of doing all of this and just look at it \nthrough the cold dead eyes of corporate strategy,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168000,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these layoffs have still been doing real damage \nto the companies making them. The most immediate  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 172480,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "damage is just to workplace morale, which I know \nmight sound a little bit wishy-washy, but in roles  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 177840,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that rely on talent in highly collaborative \nenvironments like tech development, it can  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 181840,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be a big deal. Workplace researchers have observed \nwhat they have dubbed turnover contagion. The idea  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 187600,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is that when a round of layoffs goes through, the \nsurvivors start updating their resumes and people  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 191840,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "who are already thinking about quitting naturally \nbecome slightly more motivated to make that jump.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 196400,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Andrea Durler, who leads research at the workplace \nanalytics firm Vizier, describes this whole cycle  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 201280,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as a clear failure of workforce planning, which is \nthe polite academic way of saying these companies  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 205920,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "keep firing people they still need. The second \nproblem is that especially in technical roles,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 210800,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a lot of doing the job well just comes down \nto having done the job for a long enough time.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 215440,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Institutional knowledge like which workarounds \nactually matter and which cursed legacy code  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220000,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "should never be touched and even really basic \nthings like who sits at what desk makes a big  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 224240,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "difference in how quickly stuff gets done. In \ntheory, companies like to imagine that this gets  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 228800,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "captured in a handover dock. In reality, even \nif the laid-off workers really did want to put  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 233520,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their all into teaching their replacement how to \ndo their job, it still doesn't. I will get into  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 239040,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this particular example in detail a little bit \nlater, but Ford recently had to hire back a team  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 243760,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of senior quality assurance engineers charmingly \nreferred to as the Greybeards to address certain  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 250080,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "failings in the company's production. The \ncompany had attempted to replace them with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 253680,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an AI system overseen by a much smaller team of \nmore technically qualified but significantly less  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 259360,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "experienced operators. And well, credit where \ncredit is due, the company reversed this move,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 264400,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "acknowledging the problem that there was nobody \nleft who could tell the AI why it was being  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 268080,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dumb. Now, this is a problem because when a \ncompany eventually realizes it cut too deep,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 273520,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "getting people back usually costs a lot more \nthan keeping them around in the first place.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 278080,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If a company is rehiring someone, that normally \nmeans they were a talented employee and they've  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 282640,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "usually landed somewhere else. So businesses are \npaying a premium on top of the recruiter fees and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 287760,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "layoff expense to get people back who for obvious \nreasons don't necessarily want to work for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 292400,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "companies who have a reputation for letting people \ngo. Companies like Meta have developed such a bad  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 297360,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reputation for morale turnovers and layoffs \nthat they are now paying a measurable premium  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301760,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to hire new staff because if given a choice, \npeople would take almost any other option for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 306640,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the same level of pay. More broadly, according to \nindustry estimates, laid-off roles are now being  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 311760,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "refilled at a significant premium from what the \noriginal position paid in the first place. Some  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 316480,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the people they want back also just simply \naren't coming back at all. Laid-off employees  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 321280,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "naturally realize that their employer doesn't \nand didn't really care about them. Of course,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 325120,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that was probably always true, but actually being \nlaid off makes it a bit harder to ignore. Support  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 330560,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "groups for laid-off Googlers reportedly ballooned \nafter 2023 cuts, full of people who hadn't written  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 335840,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "resumes in 15 years because they genuinely \nbelieved they found the last job they would  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 339760,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ever need. Now, I know there's a level of irony \nin these people losing their extremely highly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 344160,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "compensated jobs, often developing the tech that \nreplaced them, especially since, let's be honest  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 349280,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "here, a lot of them would happily automate other \njobs if it meant a slightly more lucrative RSU  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 354480,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "package. But the point here is that purely from \nthe business perspective, if layoffs were supposed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 359760,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to cut costs, they haven't been doing a very good \njob. This is also ignoring things like severance,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 365200,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "higher unemployment insurance rates, and recruiter \nfees to enable the constant churn. The losses  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 370080,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "aren't random either. Stanford economist Nick \nBloom pointed out that attrition driven by return  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 374960,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to office mandates loses companies or best people \nfirst because the best people are the ones with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 379440,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "outside options. What's left is mostly the people \nwho couldn't afford to leave. Now, obviously,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 384480,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mandatory return to office is slightly different \nfrom layoffs, but they were done with the same  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 388800,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "intention of thinning out the workforce, hoping \nthat people would quit instead of publicly having  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 392960,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to reduce headcount. Now, it sounds dumb, but \nit gets dumber. So far, these have just been the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 398640,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "immediate costs. Entry-level hiring has collapsed \nby somewhere between 55 and 65% at the major tech  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 404800,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "companies since the layoff era began. IBM's head \nof HR, Nicol Maro, has been unusually blunt,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 410960,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "warning that if you cut the pipeline, there's \nno secession talent in 3 to 5 years. And IBM is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 416160,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "now tripling its entry-level hiring to patch the \nhole it dug for itself. And on top of all of that,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 421200,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these companies are burning the thing that made \nthem special employers in the first place. A job  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 425600,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at Google used to be the prize. Partly this was \nbecause of the pay, which at the time was decent  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 430640,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but still inferior to something like finance, \nbut mostly because it was seen as the best  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 435200,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "place in the world to work. It had cool offices, \ninteresting projects, a decent work life balance,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 440560,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a vibe culture, and most importantly, good job \nsecurity. If that perception dies, the smartest  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 446560,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "people can just take their talents to finance \ninstead, which pays comparably, never pretended to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 451520,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "love you, and is somehow now the less risky option \nfor a high performer who doesn't want to find out  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 456480,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from a LinkedIn notification that they no longer \nwork there. Now, to play devil's advocate here,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 462480,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "despite the beanag chairs and kombucha on tap, \nthese are still for-profit companies, not a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 467520,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "jobs program. Even if there are costs associated \nwith doing layoffs, and there are a lot of costs,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 473200,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it could be argued that endlessly accumulating \nstaff who feel overly secure in the role would  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 478720,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "end up costing a business more, especially if all \ntheir competitors are willing to pay a premium to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 483040,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "poach their best talent. But well, these companies \nare still accumulating workers. Alphabet finished  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 489360,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "2025 with around 190,000 employees, essentially \nback to its all-time peak. Microsoft ended its  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 495600,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "last fiscal year with 228,000 people, identical \nto the year before, despite cutting roughly 15,000  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 502000,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "across two heavily publicized rounds. And Amazon \nis within 2% of its 2021 high even after 4 years  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 508480,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of going allin on headcount efficiency. So, the \nworkforce is right back to where it started. And  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 513440,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "since most entry-level hiring has been so slow, \nthis pool of workers has mostly been maintained  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 517840,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with people just moving to the next company in \nline. So they did all of this, accounted for all  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 523200,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of these costs, accepted the human toll, took the \nmorale and reputational hits, all to effectively  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 528160,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just play past the parcel of employees. Which \nraises the obvious question of why? Well,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 533920,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Stanford business professor Jeffrey Feffer \nhas spent decades on this exact question, and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 538240,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "his conclusion has been depressingly consistent. \nLayoffs repeatedly failed to cut costs or improve  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 543200,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "performance once you account for severance, morale \ndamage, and lost productivity. The same was true  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 547680,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for headcount trimming efforts like return to \noffice mandates. Researchers at the University  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 551840,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of Pittsburgh's Catz Business School went looking \nfor financial improvement at S\u0026P 500 companies  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 556800,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that imposed strict return to office mandates \nand found no significant improvement at all.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 561360,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Employee satisfaction, on the other hand, dropped \nmeasurably. Now, say what you want about the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 565840,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "senior leadership of these companies. Most of them \naren't stupid. They are capable of reading these  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 570800,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "studies and understanding the basic cost benefit \nof what they are doing. But across corporate  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 575040,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "America and particularly in tech, they are doing \nit anyway for three simple reasons. The first is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 580560,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quite simply human vindictiveness. For the last \ndecade in particular, tech workers had a little  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 585120,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bit too much power and security, safe in the fact \nthat nobody else could do what they do, which  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 589840,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bred a culture very different from a standard \ncorporate hierarchy. Staff pushed social agendas,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 594560,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spoke out against their own bosses, worked their \nown schedules, and demanded recognition for their  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 599040,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "achievements. You know, a true nightmare scenario. \nA lot of these companies leaned into this idea  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 603920,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when it was convenient for talent acquisition. \nBut it has become pretty clear that the actual  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 607920,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "executives at the top of these businesses, not so \nsecretly, despised this culture. People like Zuck  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 613120,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "may have been the CEO of one of the most powerful \ncompanies on Earth. But his entitled employees  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 617600,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "didn't kiss the ground around his feet like \nthey ought to in a more traditional company. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 621440,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Wall Street Journal ran what might as well be the \nera's mission statement as a headline. The bosses  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 625920,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are back in charge. Executives saw the layoff \nwave as a chance to put entitled workers back  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 630880,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in their place after the leverage workers built \nup during the great resignation and the remote  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 635040,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "work boom. The commentator Ed Zitron went further \nin a piece bluntly titled tech elite hates labor,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 641120,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "arguing that the industry resents having created \na pampered class of worker that it now has to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 645520,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cater to and overpay. Even several prominent \npeople within tech have been happy to say the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 650480,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quiet part out loud. Venture capitalist Keith \nRabo boy said Meta and Google hired thousands  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 655120,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of people to do fake work out of vanity called \nthose workers extraneous and held up Elon Musk's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 660320,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Twitter purge where roughly 80% of the company \nwas cut as the model the rest of the industry  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 664400,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "should copy. Venture capitalists and real life \nBaron from Dune. Mark Andre mocked the remote  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 669600,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "laptop class. David Saxs called Twitter bloated \nand overstaffed. All of this meant that when  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 674800,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass layoffs became more commonplace across the \nindustry and these executives saw that their stock  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 678960,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "price wouldn't take a hit for doing it and in many \ncases would actually trend upwards, a lot of them  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 683680,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "were more than happy to dive in, even if the \nlong-term benefits would be questionable. Sure,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 689520,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they might have just ended up passing the same \npeople along to the next company, but it did still  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 694080,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "send the message that nobody is irreplaceable. The \nHR software company Bamboo HR surveyed more than  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 699440,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "1500 managers and found that roughly a quarter \nof sea suite executives openly admitted that they  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 703600,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hoped return to office mandates would get people \nto quit voluntarily. About 20% of HR leaders said  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 709040,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their inoff rules were specifically designed to \ndrive attrition. And around 40% of managers said  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 714400,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "layoffs followed because not enough people left on \ntheir own. And again, this was just what they were  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 719520,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "publicly admitting to. I don't get it. Why are \nthey confessing? They're not confessing. They're  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 726880,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bragging. The sad part is it kind of worked. \nIn one survey, the share of workers who said  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 732560,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they would quit over a mandatory return to office \ncollapsed from 51% to just 7% in a single year.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 738640,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Again, this is just one variable. But ultimately, \npeople have now been trained to hold on to their  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 743600,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "jobs at all cost. It's not great, but even \nthe most vindictive CEO still needs to justify  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 748960,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their smiting to shareholders who don't have \nendless patience for workplace power politics.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 753600,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, it's time to learn How many works to find \nout how they have actually justified this so  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 757600,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "far and why it's kind of backfiring on them. This \nvideo is sponsored by ODO. ODU is the all-in-one  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 763840,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "business management platform that pulls sales, \naccounting, inventory, manufacturing, e-commerce,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 768480,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and CRM into a single connected system instead of \nforcing you to juggle five different tools that do  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 773120,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not talk to each other. What makes ODU work is how \nseamlessly it ties everything together. You only  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 778160,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "install the apps you actually need and they share \ndata automatically. A sale in your storefront  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 782800,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "updates inventory and accounting in real time, so \nyou do not have to manually update your different  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 787440,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "systems, and you get to stay more organized. We \nuse ODO ourselves. Our works library runs entirely  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 793760,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on ODO, and having everything in one dashboard has \nmade the day-to-day so much smoother. It is also  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 799440,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "intuitive and highly customizable, which makes \nit a good fit for small to mediumsiz businesses  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 804080,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that have limited technical expertise and want \nsoftware that adapts to how they work instead of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 808480,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the other way around. And because ODU automates \nthe routine stuff, invoicing, restock alerts,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 813360,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "follow-ups, you spend less time doing admin or \nstitching tools together, and more time actually  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 818400,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "running your business. If you want to see how \nODU can streamline your own business, you can  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 822480,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "book a free meeting with an ODO expert or try the \nfull platform free for 15 days with no credit card  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 827600,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "required or even keep a single app free for life. \nCheck it out using my link in the description.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 833040,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So big tech bosses with bruised egos finally \ngetting one up on the people who actually  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 837600,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "built their technology in the first place is \nnice and all, but outside of their emotional  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 841760,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "support podcasts, this kind of justification isn't \ngoing to fly. Expensive and financially damaging  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 847280,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "layoffs still need to hold up to the scrutiny from \nshareholders. But well, if anything, they have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 852000,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "been right on board, largely because of the equity \nmath. A huge chunk of tech compensation is paid in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 857760,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "restricted stock units or RSUs. Grants of company \nshares that drip out to an employee over about  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 863200,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "four years depending on the exact agreement, but \nonly as long as they stick around long enough to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 867840,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "collect them. This has been a great deal for these \ncompanies because it allows them to pay worldclass  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 872720,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "engineers worldclass compensation with shares it \ncan print rather than money it has to earn. And it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 878080,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "also means that staff are compelled to stay with \ntheir company to actually receive shares they have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 882880,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "technically already earned. But printing shares \ncreates its own problem. Every RSU that eventually  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 888240,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "vests is a brand new share, and every brand new \nshare makes everyone else's slice of the company  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 893200,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a little thinner. Shareholders are uh not fans of \ngetting thinner. Now, over the last decade, the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 899760,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "solution to this has been stock buyback programs \nlargely just to mop this up. The company takes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 904720,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "real cash and buys its own shares off the open \nmarket to cancel out the new ones being minted for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 909280,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "staff. Analysts have estimated that over the last \nseveral years, effectively all of Meta's roughly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 914080,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "$96 billion of buybacks and around 90% of Google's \n156 billion went to sterilizing the dilution from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 921040,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "employee stock compensation. So yeah, effectively \nthey were just paying their staff out of their  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 925680,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "retained earnings in a very roundabout way. \nEventually shareholders realized that this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 930000,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would all work a lot better, at least in the short \nterm, if companies kept doing buybacks while doing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 934640,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "less stock creation for their employees. When \nsomeone gets cut before their RSUs finish vesting,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 939920,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the unvested shares just disappear. They were \nnever created. So, the dilution never happens,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 945600,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the company even gets to reverse the expense \nit had already booked. In normal times, this would  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 950160,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mean that more of the buyback money could go to \nbidding up the price of stock in regular markets.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 954880,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But well, we are not really in normal times right \nnow. All of the money that was going to buybacks  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 960080,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to offset these stock bonuses is now going to \ndata centers instead. Now, I have already spoken  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 965840,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "endlessly about this spending, so I am not going \nto waste your time again. But the point is that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 970240,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they can either spend money on stock buybacks, \nspend money on neutralizing RSUs, or spend money  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 975120,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on data centers. They don't have enough to do \nall three. Google and Meta have already started  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 980240,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "scaling back their buyback programs. And some \nof these companies are now issuing debt to fund  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 984480,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "data center construction or going even further and \nactually issuing new shares onto public markets.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 989840,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For existing shareholders, this made them much \nmore amendable to a level of employee churn to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 994080,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "avoid employees actually collecting their stock \nto sell on public markets. Most of the big firms  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 998800,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "vest RSUs at a flat rate of about 25% per year. \nSo, if you earn $200,000 in stock compensation  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1004720,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in a given year, you get $50,000 after the \nfirst 12 months, $50,000 12 months after that,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1010320,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and so on. But if you keep working there, \nthose future bonuses keep stacking up. So,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1014800,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even assuming your bonuses stay totally flat, \nif you quit, you are still walking away from as  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1019200,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "much as $500,000 in compensation that you will \nhave theoretically already earned. And again,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1025040,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this is in companies that spread things evenly. \nAmazon schedule is infamously backloaded at 5, 15,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1031280,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "40, and 40%. So, an Amazon employee cut 2 years \nin has collected just 20% of the equity they were  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1037760,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "promised. People have started reading these \nschedules very carefully for reasons that are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1042240,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "probably obvious by now. In a culture of permanent \nchurn, a lot of these RSUs simply never live  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1047360,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "long enough to vest. Now, if you hypothetically \nwanted to be a bit cynical, according to company  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1052720,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "financials, there are currently over a quarter of \na trillion dollars in outstanding stock waiting to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1056960,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be accessed by employees in just the top seven \ntech companies alone. That's also only as of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1061920,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the date those bonuses were actually granted. If \nyou get awarded 100 shares worth $50,000 and then  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1067200,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the price doubles next year before it's actually \nhanded over to you to liquidate, you still get 100  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1072000,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "shares. they are just now worth $100,000. Now, of \ncourse, in theory, this is a nice way to motivate  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1078000,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "staff to align them behind a stock price, but \nit also means that the true value of outstanding  "
    } ]
  }, {
